# K-EXAONE-2.0 + the Spark node tier: why the node leg does not hide (round nodeasync, from exaone-spark-1114.card's
# outcome, 9.59 tok/s = 104.3 ms a step against the overlapped 13.9..15.2).
# Order today (main 4fe5efe1f, captured Chain::Step, read off the code): card go(L) -> host take_go (step.rs:1075) -> picks read
# -> NodePoint::Picks (step.rs:1134) -> TierNode::send_enqueue (tier.rs:2306, node.rs:1408, no reply awaited) and
# TierPage::add_go (mod.rs:3522) -> the pump posts the image (link.rs:358) -> host experts (step.rs:1186/1188) -> signal
# (step.rs:1222). The only wait on the node is the stage card's join wait (tier.rs:965 enqueue_back_of); the host waits on it
# once a step, after its last layer (mod.rs:3175 settle_tiers). So the node leg already starts before the host leg and is
# joined only at the card's join: the code's order is the overlapped one, and the serial reading is not an order of calls.
# Per layer [derived, exaone-spark-model.py terms]: card front 0.211 ms + PCIe 0.022 in series; host CPU 3.91 reads x 151 us =
# 0.59 ms (+0.02 picks, frame, add_go); host DRAM 53 MB = 0.40 ms at 134 GB/s, under the CPU; link 24.6 KB out + 196.6 KB of
# whole reply rows back = 178 us on the wire + 52 us RTT; GB10 42.5 MB = 0.20..0.27 ms; node leg 0.42..0.56; NVMe 0. Wall =
# front + max(host, node) + PCIe: 17.7 + 76 x 0.61..0.65 = 64..67 ms. The reading, 104.3 ms, puts 1.14 ms a layer on the max.
# The node-arm-only resource the paper left out: the TCP end's pump thread spins for the link's life (link.rs:358, yield_now
# loop) and its receiver busy-polls 50 us a read (tcp.rs:80); both float (Placement::Float) and, with the pool on every
# physical core (BLOOMERY_THREADS unset = 32), land on SMT siblings of pool participants. The pool's chunks are static and each
# dispatch ends at a barrier (crates/threads/src/lib.rs:16-18; :266-270 names this hazard for the dispatcher), so one
# participant sharing its core gates every host-leg barrier: a slowdown s of that participant makes the leg 0.61 / (1 - s);
# s = 0.46 gives the measured 1.14 ms. Plan a and bp (shared page, no pump) have no such thread: bp read its derived +5.8 %.
# Two causes fit 1.14 ms: (H3) the host leg taxed by the link threads, the node hidden; (H2) the node leg itself ~1.1 ms (GB10
# rate or wire below the model), the host leg 0.61. A tok/s re-read cannot split them; one pool core left free can: at
# BLOOMERY_THREADS=31 the floating pump and receiver take the free core (the scheduler spreads to an idle core before a
# sibling), the leg costs 32/31: 0.63 + 0.02 (+ <= 0.06 of RX softirq on a participant) -> 17.7 + 76 x 0.65..0.71 =
# 67.1..71.7 ms, 13.9..14.9 tok/s under H3 [derived]; under H2 the bound is the node and the arm reads the base -1..+1 %.
kind: baseline
question: does leaving one physical core free of the pool (BLOOMERY_THREADS=31) let K-EXAONE-2.0's decode with the Spark node
  tier reach the overlapped rate, i.e. is the node arm's lost gain the link threads' tax on the host leg
term: tg tok/s of the THREADS=31 node arm @ n=127, prompt 4096 corpus-prose ids, ctx 8192 one slot, A6000 + host + the Spark
  over TCP, draft off; read against the default node arm's 9.59 (exaone-spark-1114.card, the same file, plan and host code)
unit: tok/s
predict: 13.2..15.2
condition: exaone-spark-1114.card's condition on one bloomery-serve binary (tree arm), the arm alone in each run, run twice; the arm's env
  env=BLOOMERY_TIER_LINK=tcp,BLOOMERY_TIER_NODES=10.41.0.2:9001,BLOOMERY_CARD_BUDGET=100G,BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off, plus
  BLOOMERY_THREADS=31; DEPTH_SERVE_PLACE=bp with the A6000 the one visible card, BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=1 (one arm a run: a two-arm run cannot wait for the node to free a load between servers), DEPTH_SERVE_CTX=8192,
  DEPTH_SERVE_N=128; the Spark's tier_server frees each load before the next arm opens (exaone-spark-1114's r1 mtp arm met a
  node still holding the last load); once per arm, mid-window, `ps -L -o tid,psr,comm -p <serve pid>` names the cpus of
  tier-link-pump and tier-link-tcp (THREADS=31: on the core no pool cpu holds; default: a pool participant's sibling); both arms'
  plan lines print card_experts=2319 host_experts=9513
reason: the overlapped model's 13.9..15.2 tok/s (exaone-spark-1114.card) is the rate with the host leg at its own speed; H3 says
  the default arm loses 0.5 ms a layer to the link threads on the pool's SMT siblings, so the arm that frees one core gets the
  overlapped rate less one pool thread's share (1/32 of the host leg) [derived]
decide-in: the link threads' tax is the cause: a round takes the TCP end's pump and receiver off the pool's cores while a
  remote link is attached (the pool leaves one core to the link and pins its helpers there, or the pump parks between the
  host's add_go and the receiver's landing), predicted to keep this arm's rate; the RDMA end (rdma.rs:182) gets the same
decide-below: the node leg is the bound in the measured remainder: read the GB10's per-layer tier time (nsys on the Spark's
  tier_server, the layer's kernels and copies) and the node rows' wire; then price the packed reply (EXAONE's ReplyRows::Whole
  sends 8 rows where the node computed 3.1) before any host lever
decide-above: read both arms' plan lines, witness blocks and the ps snapshot before crediting; a default arm below 9.4 means
  the base window was disturbed, not that the lever gained more
# outcome 2026-11-14: one row, not a verdict. r1 THREADS=31: 7.74 tok/s (pp 43.20, load 248 s) with 572,775 major faults [cold],
#  against the default arm's 9.59 at 12,705: the host's page cache had been displaced (the IQ1_S mix's and the loss runs' loads
#  between the two sittings), so the row reads the host's page-ins, not the pool. r2 did not open: the Spark's tier_server refused
#  the open with cuDevicePrimaryCtxRetain out of memory although MemAvailable was over 112 GB (the loop's wait): GB10's device
#  memory comes back later than MemAvailable shows. Next, before another node run: tier_server waits for (or retries) the
#  device context on open, and the node arm's window starts with the host set warm (the default arm re-read in the same hold).
